item.description:
The 87-year-old pensioner in Spain whose eviction from her lifelong home sparked nationwide protests has died. María del Carmen Abascal, better known as Maricarmen, died on Wednesday just hours before she was set to sign a new rental agreement. She was forcibly removed from her Madrid apartment last month on a stretcher after the private investment company that owns the building hiked her rent. After massive public outcry, the landlord had agreed to allow her back into her apartment.</p>
<p>Independent Spanish journalist María Carrión says that although Maricarmen&#8217;s story galvanized much of the country, evictions like hers are &#8220;a daily occurrence&#8221; in Spain.</p>
<p>&#8220;Rents have gone up 80% in the last 10 years, while salaries have not kept up,&#8221; Carrión says. &#8220;Young people cannot leave their homes. They cannot become independent or start families. And this is what people are trying to change from the street.&#8221;

Tennessee&#8217;s Governor Bill Lee has halted executions for the remainder of the year after a death row prisoner survived two lethal injections on Wednesday. According to her lawyers, Christa Pike was still breathing after she was strapped to a gurney and injected with two syringes of pentobarbital in a botched execution that spanned more than two hours. Prior to the scheduled execution, Pike and her lawyers argued a single-dose lethal injection could be tantamount to torture due to Pike&#8217;s small veins. Pike was sentenced to death for a 1995 murder she committed when she was 18. Now in the hospital in critical condition, she is the first person to ever survive lethal injection in the United States.</p>
<p>&#8220;I have seen five executions in Tennessee,&#8221; says Steven Hale, a criminal justice reporter for the <em>Nashville Banner</em>, who witnessed Christa Pike&#8217;s botched execution. &#8220;And I&#8217;ve never seen anything like what we saw Wednesday night.&#8221;</p>
<p>Christa Pike &#8220;has just experienced the most agonizing attempt at lethal injection in the country&#8217;s history,&#8221; says Sandra Babcock, director of the Cornell Center on the Death Penalty Worldwide. Babcock says Pike should have never been sentenced to death, because she was an &#8220;immature 18-year-old&#8221; with a history of severe childhood and sexual trauma who &#8220;was not receiving medication for post-traumatic stress disorder and bipolar disorder at the time of [the murder], which she committed with her abusive boyfriend at the time.&#8221;

The Palestinian death toll from Israel&#8217;s war on Gaza has surpassed 74,000, according to the Ministry of Health in Gaza. While this figure represents the official death toll as confirmed by local authorities, the true number could be much higher when missing people and indirect deaths are taken into account, such as from disease, exposure, denial of medical treatment and the destruction of civic infrastructure. And since the so-called ceasefire brokered by Trump last October, nearly 1,400 Palestinians have been killed in Israeli attacks.</p>
<p>&#8220;The [ceasefire] plan ended up just buying more time for the genocide,&#8221; says Muhammad Shehada, a writer and analyst from Gaza speaking to <em>Democracy Now!</em> in Copenhagen. &#8220;Instead of suspending attacks on Gaza, Israel has been bombing Gaza every day.&#8221;</p>
<p>&#8220;The idea of the plan was to divide the reconstruction, rehabilitation, Israeli withdrawal, decommissioning of Hamas and Palestinian statehood into three phases,&#8221; says Shehada. &#8220;So far, only one item of the 20 items on the Trump plan has been fulfilled, and that is the release of every single Israeli hostage in Gaza.&#8221;

2026 is already the deadliest year for measles in the United States since the virus was declared eliminated in 2000. Four people died over the last month in Pennsylvania amid an ongoing outbreak in the state, which had not seen a measles-related death in at least 35 years. All four victims, including two infants, were unvaccinated (children do not receive their first dose of the measles vaccine until they have reached a year old). The deaths come as President Trump and Health and Human Services Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. continue to defund public vaccination programs and push debunked claims that vaccines cause autism.</p>
<p>Pediatrician and vaccine expert Dr. Paul Offit warns that the four reported measles deaths — which were reported by Pennsylvania health officials but have not yet been acknowledged by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention — are likely a vast undercount. He estimates that there could be &#8220;closer to 4,000&#8221; cases in the Pennsylvania outbreak. &#8220;I don&#8217;t think people realize how contagious this virus is,&#8221; Offit says. &#8220;You don&#8217;t have to have direct contact with somebody who has measles. You just have to be within their airspace within two hours of them being there.&#8221;</p>
<p>We also speak to Offit about the <span class="caps">COVID</span>-19 virus, which he says is now affecting the United States &#8220;like flu&#8221; by causing &#8220;hundreds of thousands of people to be hospitalized and tens of thousands to die, just like influenza does, every year.&#8221; Yearly flu and <span class="caps">COVID</span>-19 vaccinations, he explains, are &#8220;effective at keeping people out of the hospital, effective at keeping them out of the intensive care unit, and effective at keeping them out of the morgue.&#8221;

Two men deported from the United States to Equatorial Guinea were arrested by local police forces and transferred to a prison earlier this month, in apparent retaliation for speaking out about conditions they faced in the Central African country. Samson Birhane, who is from Eritrea, and Ahmed Soliman, a gay asylum seeker from Egypt, were both deported by the Trump administration despite U.S. immigration courts granting them protections.</p>
<p>On September 11, police forces arrived at Hotel Bamy in the city of Malabo, where more than two dozen people have been confined after their deportation from the United States to Equatorial Guinea, a country they have no ties to, as part of President Trump&#8217;s secretive <a href="https://www.democracynow.org/2026/9/1/trump_admin_third_country_deportations">&#8220;third-country&#8221; agreements</a>. Birhane and Soliman say they were arrested and taken to prison for speaking out about the inhumane conditions endured by U.S. deportees at the hotel and threats by armed forces. While confined at the hotel, U.S. deportees have decried denial of medical care and legal assistance, and abuses they&#8217;ve described as psychological torture.</p>
<p>&#8220;Starting in November 2025, … the United States started this horrendous policy of sending immigrants not from Equatorial Guinea to Equatorial Guinea, a country that the U.S. knows very, very well that is a human rights violator,&#8221; says human rights lawyer Tutu Alicante. &#8220;The State Department reports that the U.S. has been putting out for the last three decades are consistent about the fact that Equatorial Guinea uses torture, uses incommunicado detentions, and often extrajudicial killings of people that it keeps in prison.&#8221;
